//! Statistics on core's `Array` and `List`.
//!
//! `stats` covers what a first statistics course does and a good part of
//! what comes after: summaries, probability distributions, random samples,
//! hypothesis tests, regression and the bootstrap. Each function takes any
//! sequence of numbers (a `Vector<f64>`, a `List`, a fixed array, a range),
//! and the results are plain structs whose fields you read by name.
//!
//! # Tutorial
//!
//! ## Summaries
//!
//! `mean`, `median`, `var`, `std`, `quantile` and the rest take the data
//! and return a number. Descriptive statistics never throw: an empty input
//! gives NaN, as in NumPy. `stats::nan::` has the same functions skipping
//! NaN values.
//!
//! ```nabi
//! let x = [5.1, 4.9, 5.6, 5.8, 6.0, 5.4, 5.2, 5.7];
//! println(f"mean {stats::mean(x):.4}, median {stats::median(x):.2}");
//! println(f"var {stats::var(x, ddof: 1):.4}, std {stats::std(x, ddof: 1):.4}");
//! println(f"90th percentile {(try stats::quantile(x, 0.9)):.3}");
//! println(f"skew {stats::skew(x):.3}");
//!
//! let gaps = [1.0, f64::NAN, 3.0];
//! println(f"{stats::mean(gaps)} {stats::nan::mean(gaps)}");
//! # assert(f"{stats::mean(x):.4} {stats::median(x):.2}" == "5.4625 5.50");
//! # assert(f"{stats::var(x, ddof: 1):.4} {stats::std(x, ddof: 1):.4}" == "0.1427 0.3777");
//! # assert(f"{(try stats::quantile(x, 0.9)):.3} {stats::skew(x):.3}" == "5.860 -0.097");
//! # assert(f"{stats::mean(gaps)} {stats::nan::mean(gaps)}" == "NaN 2");
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! mean 5.4625, median 5.50
//! var 0.1427, std 0.3777
//! 90th percentile 5.860
//! skew -0.097
//! NaN 2
//! ```
//!
//! `quantile` interpolates as NumPy does by default; the `method:` argument
//! picks any of the Hyndman and Fan definitions when a field expects
//! another.
//!
//! ## Distributions
//!
//! A distribution is a value: `Normal::new(100.0, 15.0)` is the normal with
//! mean 100 and standard deviation 15. Continuous distributions have `pdf`,
//! `cdf`, `sf` (one minus the cdf, accurate in the tail), `ppf` (the
//! inverse cdf) and `interval`; discrete ones have `pmf` instead of `pdf`.
//!
//! ```nabi
//! use stats::{Binomial, Continuous, Discrete, Normal};
//!
//! let iq = try Normal::new(100.0, 15.0);
//! println(f"P(IQ > 130) = {iq.sf(130.0):.4}");
//! println(f"middle 95%: {iq.interval(0.95)}");
//! println(f"97.5th percentile {iq.ppf(0.975):.2}");
//!
//! let coin = try Binomial::new(10, 0.5);
//! println(f"P(7 heads of 10) = {coin.pmf(7):.4}, P(at most 7) = {coin.cdf(7):.4}");
//! # assert(f"{iq.sf(130.0):.4} {iq.ppf(0.975):.2}" == "0.0228 129.40");
//! # assert(f"{iq.interval(0.95)}" == "[70.6005402318992, 129.3994597681008]");
//! # assert(f"{coin.pmf(7):.4} {coin.cdf(7):.4}" == "0.1172 0.9453");
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! P(IQ > 130) = 0.0228
//! middle 95%: [70.6005402318992, 129.3994597681008]
//! 97.5th percentile 129.40
//! P(7 heads of 10) = 0.1172, P(at most 7) = 0.9453
//! ```
//!
//! ## Random samples
//!
//! A `Sampler` is a seeded random number generator: the same seed gives the
//! same draws on every machine, which makes a simulation repeatable.
//! `d.samples(ref g, n)` draws `n` values from `d`.
//!
//! ```nabi
//! use stats::{Continuous, Distribution, Normal, Sampler};
//!
//! let g = Sampler::seed(7);
//! let d = try Normal::new(10.0, 2.0);
//! let xs = d.samples(ref g, 10000);
//! println(f"{xs.len()} draws: mean {stats::mean(xs):.2}, std {stats::std(xs, ddof: 1):.2}");
//!
//! let fitted = try Normal::fit(xs);
//! println(f"fitted mean {fitted.mean():.2}");
//! # assert(f"{xs.len()} {stats::mean(xs):.2} {stats::std(xs, ddof: 1):.2}" == "10000 10.00 1.99");
//! # assert(f"{fitted.mean():.2}" == "10.00");
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! 10000 draws: mean 10.00, std 1.99
//! fitted mean 10.00
//! ```
//!
//! `fit` estimates a distribution's parameters by maximum likelihood; most
//! distributions in the package have one.
//!
//! ## Hypothesis tests
//!
//! A test returns its statistic and p-value. The t-tests also return the
//! estimate and its standard error, so the confidence interval is one more
//! call (`ci`). Here a drug trial: did the treated group's blood pressure
//! fall more than the control group's?
//!
//! ```nabi
//! let control = [-2.1, 0.5, -1.0, 1.3, -0.4, 0.8, -1.7, 0.2, -0.6, 1.1];
//! let treated = [-6.3, -4.1, -7.5, -2.9, -5.2, -3.8, -6.9, -4.4, -5.8, -3.1];
//!
//! let t = try stats::ttest_ind(treated, control);
//! println(f"difference {t.estimate:.2} mmHg, 95% interval {t.ci(0.95)}");
//! println(f"p = {t.pvalue:.2e}, significant at 1%: {t.result().rejects(0.01)}");
//!
//! // Are the treated values plausibly normal? Shapiro-Wilk.
//! let sw = try stats::shapiro(treated);
//! println(f"Shapiro-Wilk p = {sw.pvalue:.3}");
//!
//! // The same question without assuming normality.
//! let u = try stats::mannwhitneyu(treated, control);
//! println(f"Mann-Whitney p = {u.pvalue:.2e}");
//! # assert(f"{t.estimate:.2} {t.ci(0.95)}" == "-4.81 [-6.131505142918329, -3.48849485708167]");
//! # assert(f"{t.pvalue:.2e}" == "7.45e-7" && t.result().rejects(0.01));
//! # assert(f"{sw.pvalue:.3} {u.pvalue:.2e}" == "0.696 1.83e-4");
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! difference -4.81 mmHg, 95% interval [-6.131505142918329, -3.48849485708167]
//! p = 7.45e-7, significant at 1%: true
//! Shapiro-Wilk p = 0.696
//! Mann-Whitney p = 1.83e-4
//! ```
//!
//! The package also has paired and one-sample t-tests, chi-squared tests of
//! fit and of contingency tables, Fisher's exact test, Kolmogorov-Smirnov,
//! Wilcoxon, one-way ANOVA (`f_oneway`) and the correlation tests. Each
//! takes `alternative:` for a one-sided test.
//!
//! ## Regression
//!
//! `stats::ols` fits `y = b0 + b1 x1 + ...` by least squares and reports
//! what a regression table does: coefficients, standard errors, t
//! statistics, p-values, R². Each row of `x` is one observation, each
//! column one predictor; the intercept is added for you.
//!
//! ```nabi
//! use core::array::{Matrix, Vector};
//!
//! // Fuel use (l/100 km) against mass (tonnes) and engine size (litres).
//! let x: Matrix<f64> = [[1.1, 1.2], [1.3, 1.4], [1.4, 1.6], [1.5, 2.0], [1.7, 2.0], [1.8, 2.5], [2.0, 3.0], [2.2, 3.0]];
//! let y: Vector<f64> = [5.2, 5.9, 6.3, 7.1, 7.4, 8.3, 9.2, 9.6];
//!
//! let fit = try stats::ols(x, y);
//! for (k, name) in ["intercept", "mass", "engine"].enumerate() {
//!     println(f"{name:>9}: {fit.coef[k]:>6.3} (se {fit.se[k]:.3}, p {fit.p[k]:.3})");
//! }
//! println(f"R^2 {fit.r2:.4}");
//!
//! let next: Matrix<f64> = [[1.6, 1.8]];
//! println(f"predicted for a 1.6 t car with a 1.8 l engine: {(try fit.predict(next))[0]:.2}");
//! # assert(f"{fit.coef[0]:.3} {fit.coef[1]:.3} {fit.coef[2]:.3}" == "1.684 1.807 1.319");
//! # assert(f"{fit.se[0]:.3} {fit.se[1]:.3} {fit.se[2]:.3}" == "0.155 0.259 0.138");
//! # assert(f"{fit.r2:.4} {(try fit.predict(next))[0]:.2}" == "0.9991 6.95");
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! intercept:  1.684 (se 0.155, p 0.000)
//!      mass:  1.807 (se 0.259, p 0.001)
//!    engine:  1.319 (se 0.138, p 0.000)
//! R^2 0.9991
//! predicted for a 1.6 t car with a 1.8 l engine: 6.95
//! ```
//!
//! `stats::logistic` fits a logistic regression for a yes/no outcome, and
//! `stats::polyfit` a polynomial in one variable.
//!
//! ## The bootstrap
//!
//! When no formula gives the uncertainty of a statistic, the bootstrap
//! estimates it by recomputing the statistic on many resamples of the data.
//! `stats::bootstrap` takes the data and the statistic as a function and
//! returns the estimate, its standard error and a confidence interval (BCa
//! by default).
//!
//! ```nabi
//! use core::array::Vector;
//!
//! let incomes = [21.0, 23.5, 25.0, 26.0, 28.5, 30.0, 31.0, 34.0, 38.0, 45.0, 60.0, 120.0];
//! let b = try stats::bootstrap(incomes, |v: Vector<f64>| -> f64 { stats::median(v) }, resamples: 2000, seed: 1);
//! println(f"median {b.estimate}, se {b.se:.2}, 95% interval {b.ci}");
//! # assert(f"{b.estimate} {b.se:.2} {b.ci}" == "30.5 4.09 [25, 38]");
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! median 30.5, se 4.09, 95% interval [25, 38]
//! ```
//!
//! ## Notation
//!
//! With the `ops` feature (on by default) some of this reads like a
//! statistics text: `95pct` is the number 0.95, `m ± h` is an interval, and
//! `g ~ d` draws from `d`.
//!
//! ```nabi
//! use stats::{Distribution, Normal, Sampler};
//!
//! let g = Sampler::seed(42);
//! let xs = g ~ (try Normal::new(10.0, 2.0)).iid(1000);
//! let ci = stats::mean(xs) ± 1.96 * stats::sem(xs);
//! println(f"{ci} contains 10: {10.0 in ci}");
//! println(95pct);
//! # assert(f"{ci}" == "[9.886659540274959, 10.129771738653204]" && 10.0 in ci);
//! # assert(95pct == 0.95);
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! [9.886659540274959, 10.129771738653204] contains 10: true
//! 0.95
//! ```
//!
